1. I checked the BCV of unrelated individuals mentioned in page 69 - that was from study based on cell lines ("RNA-Seq profiles were made from lymphoblastoid cell lines"). They are grown in controlled conditions, uniformly. But, in my case, the samples are tissues dissected from donors just after death. Anyway, I now filtered out the outliers by using a more stringent cut-off of "keep <- rowSums(cpm(D)>1) >= 30" and I get a BCV of 51% ("Disp = 0.26425 , BCV = 0.5141"). I have also attached the BCV plot.


2. About the ANOVA-type test: I still do not understand why the first group gets treated as the baseline. In my case, all samples (or groups) are normal. So all of these are in one sense the "wild-type". And, when the first group gets absorbed in the intercept, the comparison of gene expression is made to the first group (as it gets treated as the baseline). I thought this approach does not require one group to be used as a wild-type. 


